      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a7.7.1',
          kind: "grammar",
          refs: ['g7.7.1', 'g7.7.5', 'g7.7.8'],
          lemma: "αἰτεῖτε, ζητεῖτε, κρούετε",
          title: "Keep asking — the tense of persistence",
          body: "All three imperatives are **present tense**, which in Greek commands ongoing action: *keep asking… keep seeking… keep knocking.* This is prayer pictured not as a transaction but as a practice — the verbs escalate from speech (ask) to movement (seek) to standing at a door you cannot open yourself (knock). Two honest observations for the reader who has asked and not received. First, the promise's content is specified in v. 11: the Father gives **good things** to those who ask — not the thing asked for; Luke's parallel sharpens it to “the Holy Spirit,” which suggests the earliest tradition already understood the guarantee to be of *God's goodness*, not of outcomes. Second, the supporting argument is not “God always says yes” but an a-fortiori about *character*: if flawed fathers (“you, being evil” — the Sermon's bluntest anthropology, dropped in passing) don't hand their children stones, the Father's answers are at least not cruel. The verse promises a door that opens; it never promises the door you were pounding on.",
        },
      ],
