      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a7.16.1',
          kind: "concept",
          refs: ['g7.16.2', 'g7.15.4'],
          lemma: "ἀπὸ τῶν καρπῶν",
          title: "The fruit test — falsifiability for prophets",
          body: "How do you unmask a wolf dressed as a sheep? Not by inspecting the wool — costume is exactly what liars are good at — and not by the wearer's own claims, which is what vv. 21-22 will demolish. Jesus gives an **empirical criterion**: output over time. Καρπός in this Gospel consistently means conduct and its consequences (3:8, “fruit worthy of the turning”); the test is repeatable, public, and slow — orchards do not lie, but they take seasons to read. It is worth savouring that the Sermon's method for detecting religious fraud is essentially *evidential*: watch what a teacher's life and following actually produce — greed, wreckage, and fear, or the list Paul would later call the Spirit's fruit. For a reader wary of being manipulated by religion (a sane wariness; wolves are stipulated here as real and incoming), this verse is the charter: you are not merely permitted to evaluate the people who claim to speak for God — you are commanded to.",
        },
      ],
