      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a7.21.1',
          kind: "concept",
          refs: ['g7.21.6', 'g7.22.14', 'g7.23.7'],
          lemma: "Κύριε Κύριε … οὐδέποτε ἔγνων ὑμᾶς",
          title: "“Lord, Lord” — religion as the last disguise",
          body: "The Sermon's most unnerving paragraph is aimed not at outsiders but at the **maximally religious**: people with correct address (“Lord, Lord”), impressive résumés (prophecy, exorcism, miracles — the claims are not disputed!), and no relationship — *“I never **knew** you.”* Γινώσκω is the knowing of persons, not facts. After warning against wolves in sheep's clothing, Jesus warns of something stranger: self-deceived sheep-clothing with no one identifiable inside — religious performance so complete it fooled the performer. Notice what this does to the usual anxieties. The doubter's fear runs: *what if my faith is too weak, too questioning, too unfinished?* This text locates the danger at the opposite pole: fluent confession, spectacular works, zero acquaintance. Nowhere does the Gospel say “I never knew you” to a doubter — the Thomases and the wavering father of Mark 9 are met, not expelled. The people at risk in Matthew's warning are the ones certain they are not.",
        },
      ],
